    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green \u2014 **36 tests, 0 failures**, BUILD SUCCESS.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the per-country national-number length rule in `E164Telephone.normalize` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/E164Telephone.java`), which is the shared normalizer that `ValidateOwnerFields` uses for `POST /api/owners` (the Cp26 checkpoint `telephone-country-length`).\n\nAfter the existing digit-count bound (8\u201315), the normalizer now validates the national number against the country code:\n- `+61` (digits start with `61`) must have exactly **9** national digits.\n- `+1` (digits start with `1`) must have exactly **10** national digits.\n\nA wrong length returns `null`, which `ValidateOwnerFields` already maps to a **400** via `OwnerFieldsInvalidException`. The two branches are disjoint (`\"61\"` vs `\"1\"`), and the default-assumed `+61` path (no leading `+`, drop a single `0`) flows through the same check, so `\"0412 345 678\"` \u2192 `+61412345678` (9 national digits) still passes. All existing telephone tests (Cp02 normalize, Cp03 uniqueness) and the fixtures in `AcceptanceBase.uniqueTelephone()` remain valid.",
